WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.399 --> 00:00:03.030 align:start position:0%
 
a mother  and child forced to take refuge in a  school  just one of several azerbaijani families  who fled here from nearby villages  they're escaping heavy fighting near the  border of nagorno-karabakh  that's torn their lives apart  as we have children we were forced to  come here but we go back and  forth even if they destroy our house we  will go back  over the border in the disputed region  of nagorno-karabakh  this armenian father surveys the  wreckage  his two sons have been fighting to  protect this breakaway territory  he blames azerbaijan for the devastation  15 to 20 times a day we can't leave  basements  or shops are closed there's nothing to  eat  [Music]  it's controlled by ethnic armenian  forces  the last two weeks have seen the worst  fighting since a war over the disputed  territory  ended in 1994. russia has been mediating  between these two former soviet states  in the early hours of saturday morning a  ceasefire was announced  the republic of azerbaijan and the  republic of armenia  are starting substantive negotiations  with the aim of achieving a peaceful  settlement  as soon as possible now there's to be an  exchange of prisoners and the  repatriation of bodies  but for families like these caught up in  this conflict  their biggest dream is for a lasting  peace  now let's bring in lawrence brewers the  caucasus program  director at the london-based peace  building organization conciliation  resources  mr brewers thanks for joining me so it  sounds like the ceasefire has been  broken before it even got a chance to go  into effect with both sides accusing  each other of launching attacks does  this mean we're back to square one  yes uh as you said the ceasefire is  supposed to have been in place for two  hours now and there are reports that  fighting has continued  um i think there are a number of factors  here first of all  russia doesn't have the same leverage  that it had four years ago the last time  there was a major escalation at that  time russia brokered a ceasefire after  four days and it held  we're now 13 days in and the ceasefire  is not holding  azerbaijan i think is very concerned  about agreeing to a ceasefire and just  seeing  a refreeze without real movement uh on  on the substance of the talks and i  think there's also a paradox  of imminent peace talks each side may  feel that now is the time to  uh make advances to strengthen  negotiating positions before  real talks resume the azerbaijani  president has said that this  would be armenia's quote last chance to  settle  the conflict peacefully this has been  going on for decades why is it proving  so complicated to solve this dispute  well there's a whole range of different  factors but i think the key issue  is that the sides have diametrically  opposed uh positions  on the status of nagorno-karabakh itself  now  the uh the statement released by the  ministry of foreign affairs of russia  underlines that substantive talks should  take place according to the basic  principles  this is a reference to the peace  proposal that's been on the table  since 2007 and the fact that it's been  on the table for so long indicates that  there  is agreement on a number of background  of less pressing secondary issues but  the status of nagorno-karabakh  is the key sticking point and we also  need more  security the armenian side has been  calling for increased ceasefire  monitoring infrastructure  uh while the azerbaijani side has been  calling for for more politics and more  uh movement on the political issues at  stake and i think this is the dilemma  that the mediators face russia and  turkey have been heavily meddling in  this conflict  walk us through what's at stake here for  them  well i think this new conflict uh is  indicative  of uh broader global movements away from  a unipolar multilateral liberal  uh international order to a more  regionalized one  and there is the prospect uh that russia  and turkey  may become you know the key players as  they as they have done  in syria and libya supporting different  factions on the ground so  this hasn't been to date a proxy  conflict but there is a risk  uh that that that that may happen and so  i think that the choice for russia  essentially is whether to agree  to a kind of duopoly with turkey to  manage this conflict  or to remain embedded within a  multilateral framework  of the mints group together with the  united states and france  lawrence brewers thanks for your  analysis  thank you  you

